utation preserves Lebesgue measure (it permutes orthonormal coordinate axes); the factor Lebesgue measures are σ-finite, hence S-finite, and ℝ is real normed, so M003 identifies its whole-space integral with the iterated integral, equal to zero. Thus ∫∂_j g_j=0 and ∫(∂_j u_t)·u_{x_j}=−∫u_t·u_{x_jx_j}; all fields are integrable by M001. Differentiate the exact Frobenius energy formula: e_t=2u_t·u_{tt}+2Σ_j u_{x_j}·∂_j u_t. Finite sums commute with integration. Summing the integration-by-parts identities and using Δ_xu=Σ_j u_{x_jx_j} gives E′(r)=2∫u_t·(u_{tt}−Δ_xu). Differentiating H001 gives 2u·u_t=0, hence u·u_t=0. At the same point H002 supplies a scalar a with u_{tt}−Δ_xu=a u. Inner product with u_t gives u_t·(u_{tt}−Δ_xu)=a(u_t·u)=0. The zero integrand is integrable, so E′(r)=0. No regularity of a is used; r>0 was arbitrary.\\\"},{\\\"op\\\":\\\"add\\\",\\\"path\\\":\\\"/graph/inferences/0/applications/-\\\",\\\"value\\\":{\\\"input\\\":\\\"M005\\\",\\\"at\\\":\\\"S001\\\",\\\"bindings\\\":\\\"X=ℝⁿ, Y=ℝ, s=C, t=J, product identified with C×J.\\\",\\\"hypothesis_checks\\\":[\\\"ℝⁿ and ℝ carry their Euclidean topologies.\\\",\\\"C is compact by its witness basis H003.\\\",\\\"J=[r/2,3r/2] is compact as a closed bounded interval.\\\"]}},{\\\"op\\\":\\\"add\\\",\\\"path\\\":\\\"/graph/inferences/0/applications/-\\\",\\\"value\\\":{\\\"input\\\":\\\"M006\\\",\\\"at\\\":\\\"S001\\\",\\\"bindings\\\":\\\"α=ℝⁿ, μ=Lebesgue, 𝕜=ℝ, E=ℝ, x₀=r, s=(r/2,3r/2), F(τ)(x)=e(x,τ), F′(τ)(x)=e_t(x,τ), bound(x)=K·1_C(x).\\\",\\\"hypothesis_checks\\\":[\\\"r belongs to the open interval (r/2,3r/2), so it is a neighborhood of x₀.\\\",\\\"For every τ in that interval F(τ) is continuous, hence measurable and a.e. strongly measurable.\\\",\\\"F(r)=e(·,r) is integrable by M001 using continuity and compact support.\\\",\\\"F′(r)=e_t(·,r) is continuous and thus a.e. strongly measurable.\\\",\\\"On compact C×J the continuous e_t is bounded by finite K; outside C it vanishes. Therefore |F′(τ)(x)|